findings.body:
Password reset tokens must be unpredictable secrets. This replaces `randomBytes(32)` (256-bit CSPRNG) with `Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e12).toString(36)`, which is (1) not cryptographically secure and (2) limited to at most ~10^12 values (~40 bits), often only ~9 base-36 characters after `padStart`.

An attacker who can request resets or try tokens can brute-force or predict values that were previously infeasible.

Edge runtimes still provide a CSPRNG via the Web Crypto API — use that instead of `Math.random`:

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```

Do not ship reset/session tokens derived from `Math.random`.
